# 🟡 Level 2 – How It Works

## Password Storage

Suppose your password is:

```text
Akshay@123
```

Bad practice:

```text
Database

Akshay@123
```

If hacked:

The attacker immediately knows your password.

---

Correct method:

```text
Password

↓

SHA-256 (or a password hashing algorithm)

↓

Hash Stored
```

The system stores the hash, **not the password**.

---

## Login Process

You type:

```text
Akshay@123
```

↓

Hash generated

↓

Compared with stored hash

↓

If both hashes match

↓

Login successful

The actual password doesn't need to be stored.

---

# Common Hash Algorithms

## MD5

128-bit hash.

Example:

```text
5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99
```

❌ No longer considered secure for security-sensitive uses because collisions can be found.

---

## SHA-1

Better than MD5.

❌ Also considered broken for collision resistance and not recommended for new security applications.

---

## SHA-256

Part of the SHA-2 family.

✅ Widely used and considered secure for many applications today.

---

# Encryption vs Hashing

| Encryption               | Hashing                               |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |
| Reversible               | One-way                               |
| Uses Keys                | No decryption key                     |
| Protects Confidentiality | Verifies Integrity / Stores Passwords |
| Example: AES             | Example: SHA-256                      |

---

# Encoding vs Encryption vs Hashing

| Encoding          | Encryption               | Hashing                     |
| ----------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------- |
| Changes format    | Protects confidentiality | Creates a fingerprint       |
| Easily reversible | Reversible with key      | Not designed to be reversed |
| Example: Base64   | Example: AES             | Example: SHA-256            |

